==Old English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*uber}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*upér-}}, a comparative form of ''*upo''.

''Germanic cognates:'' Old Saxon {{term|oƀar|lang=osx}}, Dutch {{term|over|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term|ubar|lang=goh}} (whence German {{term|über|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|yfir|lang=non}} (whence Swedish {{term|över|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂|tr=ufar|lang=got}}.

''Indo-European cognates:'' Ancient Greek {{term|ὕπερ|lang=grc}}, {{term|ὑπέρ|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|super|lang=la}}, Old Irish {{term|for|lang=sga}} (Welsh {{term|gor-|lang=cy}}).

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/ˈover/|lang=ang}}

