===Etymology 2===
From Western {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*ōbaraz}}, probably ultimately from a derivative of the {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} root {{m|ine-pro|*h₂ep-||water}}. Cognate with Middle Dutch {{term|oever|lang=dum}} (Dutch {{term|oever|lang=nl}}), Middle High German {{term|uover|lang=gmh}} (German {{term|Ufer|lang=de}}). Cf. also Ancient Greek {{term|ἤπειρος|tr=ēpeiros|lang=grc}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/ˈoːver/|lang=ang}}

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|m|head=ōfer}}

# [[border]], [[edge]]; specifically, [[shore]], [[riverbank]]

